{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3276","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-contract-verification-async-reporting.md@sha256:e91428b5384a5968a35d26dbf126ba419a9d0705c3f5673e87444b3b142261b2"},"owner_reference":"3276","content":"# Feature Context: Contract-Verification Async Reporting (#3276)\n\n**Item**: #3276 \u2014 fix: contract-verification agent's findings never reach the orchestrator under async subagent dispatch\n**Type**: recurring-pattern (defect class)\n**Priority**: P2\n**Status of this document**: WHAT/WHY only. Mechanism selection is a planning decision and is deliberately not specified here.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem Space\n\n### 1.1 The observable failure\n\n`dh:contract-verification` is a post-task quality gate. It compares method signatures and type\ncontracts from the architect spec against the files a just-completed task modified, and emits a\n`<concerns>` block classifying mismatches as `CONTRACT VIOLATION` or `CONTRACT GAP`.\n\nIts instructions end with an explicit destination override\n(`plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md:151`):\n\n> Your finding is your response. The dispatcher reads it there \u2014 you register no artifact and write no task state.\n\nThat sentence assumes the caller receives the subagent's final text as a synchronous return value.\nUnder this runtime's dispatch semantics for non-fork `Agent()` calls, it does not: the dispatch\nreturns a \"Spawned successfully\" acknowledgment, and the agent's plain-text output is not visible to\nthe dispatcher. The finding is written to a channel nobody reads.\n\nThe consuming step, `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a, then\nbranches on that never-arriving value:\n\n> If the contract-verification agent returns a `<concerns>` block, append each concern to the backlog item\u2026\n\nThe branch condition is never true. Every run reports clean.\n\n### 1.2 Why this is worse than a normal bug\n\nThere is no error, no exception, no timeout, no blocked state. The dispatch call itself succeeds.\nThe absence of reported contract violations is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from \"no violations\nfound\". A designed-in quality gate is silently inert, and the only signal available to a human is\nnoticing that contract concerns never appear in any backlog item \u2014 an absence, not an event. This\nis the reason the item is P2 rather than P3: it is silent data loss inside a gate whose entire\nvalue is catching what humans miss.\n\n### 1.3 The failure is not confined to one agent\n\nA second, independent instance of the identical defect class exists in a different workflow.\n`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` (Agent Failure procedure) dispatches\n`dh:task-worker` via a bare `Agent()` call asking it to \"Report: last_success, first_failure,\nfailure_type\", then feeds those three values into a Mermaid decision tree in step 4. Verified: the\ndispatch and the branching tree are both present as described. The diagnostic values never arrive,\nso the `\"2nd failure \u2192 Escalation\"` branch is unreachable \u2014 currently dead code guarding an\nescalation path.\n\nBoth sites share one root assumption, not one root file. Fixing only the agent named in the title\nleaves the second site broken in exactly the same silent way.\n\n### 1.4 The assumption is codified as doctrine\n\n`skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md:11` states:\n\n> A dispatch that hands over no dh operation \u2014 an independent review whose finding is its response \u2014\n> has nothing here to reach\u2026\n\nThis is not a stray comment; it is the root doctrine file that authorizes \"finding is its response\"\nas a legitimate dispatch category. `contract-verification.md` is that doctrine's reference\nimplementation. As long as the doctrine stands unqualified, future agent authors will reproduce the\ndefect, and the fix to one agent decays.\n\n`skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` already carries a correct general fallback (\"every other\ndocument is an artifact, registered with `artifact_register`\u2026 Hand the next step a plan, task, or\nartifact id\"). `contract-verification.md` explicitly opts *out* of that fallback via its line-151\noverride. The general contract was never wrong; the override is.\n\n### 1.5 How the codebase arrived here\n\nTwo commits explain the state, and the order matters:\n\n- `11082f0eb` \u2014 \"refactor(development-harness): route agent handoffs through the backend, not\n  SendMessage (#3202)\" \u2014 deliberately removed `contract-verification`'s SendMessage reporting step,\n  on the sound rationale that SendMessage is a Claude-Code-only tool while this plugin's content\n  also targets Codex, OpenCode, Crush and Kilo. It replaced the removed step with \"your finding is\n  your response\" \u2014 a substitution that is correct in intent (get off SendMessage) but landed on a\n  channel that does not exist under async dispatch. The `tools:` frontmatter grant of `SendMessage`\n  was never revoked (verified: still present at `contract-verification.md:5`); only the instruction\n  to use it was removed.\n- `43058f345` \u2014 \"fix(development-harness): add SendMessage completion report to impact-analyst\n  (#3249)\" \u2014 fixed the sibling symptom for `impact-analyst` by adding\n  `SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\", \u2026)`. That fix is real but **not transferable**: `impact-analyst` is\n  dispatched via `TeamCreate`, where `team-lead` is an addressable teammate.\n  `contract-verification` is dispatched by a bare `Agent()` call from inside a skill run with no\n  team. There is no `team-lead` to address. Copying that fix verbatim would reproduce the original\n  defect in a new costume.\n\nSo this is a regression introduced by an otherwise-correct architectural refactor, not an\noversight of the #3249 fix. The item's initial hypothesis (\"appears to have been missed by that\nsame fix\") was investigated and superseded during grooming \u2014 the fact-check confirmed the change\nwas deliberate.\n\n### 1.6 Scope boundary against #3250\n\n#3250 audits `dh:*` agents dispatched **as `TeamCreate` teammates** that lack a\n`SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\", \u2026)` completion step. #3276 covers the **non-team bare-`Agent()`**\ndispatch context, where no team-lead exists at all. RT-ICA condition 7 (Decision: APPROVED)\nestablishes this as a boundary that must not be collapsed: the two items have different failure\nmechanisms, different correct fixes, and must remain independently verifiable. #3248 (impact-analyst,\nalready fixed) is related history, not a dependency.\n\nThe user has explicitly ruled out one tempting shortcut: **do not convert\n`dh:contract-verification` into a `TeamCreate` teammate merely to obtain a working report-back\nchannel.** It should remain a plain background `Agent()` spawn.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Desired Outcome\n\nWhen a bare, non-team `Agent()`-spawned `dh:*` agent finishes its analysis, its findings reach the\nstep that needs them \u2014 reliably, every run, without human transcription, and without the caller\nblocking on a message that never arrives.\n\nConcretely, and stated as outcomes rather than mechanisms:\n\n1. **Violations surface.** Contract violations found for a task become visible to the consuming\n   step (the `implement-feature` orchestrator's decision logic and/or the backlog item's durable\n   record) after the agent finishes \u2014 every time.\n2. **Clean results are distinguishable from dropped results.** A \"no violations\" outcome is\n   affirmatively recorded. Silence must never be the encoding for either state, because today\n   silence encodes both.\n3. **The second site is fixed too.** `groom/error.md`'s Agent Failure procedure receives real\n   `last_success` / `first_failure` / `failure_type` data, so its escalation branch becomes\n   reachable and acts on real values.\n4. **The doctrine stops authorizing the anti-pattern.** `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`'s \"finding is\n   its response\" category is qualified, scoped, or removed so it no longer describes a valid\n   destination for dispatch contexts where no synchronous or addressable return exists. Without\n   this, the fix is a patch on one file and the pattern regenerates.\n5. **The #3250 boundary holds.** Applying #3276's fix alone resolves both reproduction cases above\n   without touching #3250's TeamCreate scope, and #3250 remains independently verifiable.\n\n**Notably, the destination already exists.** `implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a already contains\nthe exact `backlog_groom(section=\"Concerns\", \u2026, append=True)` call that should record the finding \u2014\nverified present in the file. What is broken is *who* writes it and *when it is read*, not *where\nit goes*. Similarly, `t0-baseline-capture.md` and `tn-verification-gate.md` are bare-`Agent()`\nverification agents already routing results through `artifact_register` for a consumer to read back\n(verified: `artifact_register` present in `t0-baseline-capture.md`). Working precedent for this\ndispatch context exists inside the same plugin. The desired outcome is that\n`contract-verification` stops being the one exception.\n\n### Out of scope\n\n- Converting the agent to a `TeamCreate` teammate (explicitly excluded by the user).\n- #3250's TeamCreate-teammate SendMessage audit.\n- `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 verified clean (zero bare `Agent(` calls; its Progress Loop already\n  instructs agents to `artifact_register` and return a `STATUS: DONE` marker the orchestrator\n  verifies via artifact listing). Correct pattern; should not be touched.\n- Re-introducing SendMessage as the transport, which would undo `11082f0eb`'s cross-harness\n  portability goal.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Stakeholders\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest | What breaks for them today |\n|---|---|---|\n| Developers running `/dh:implement-feature` | Trust the step 4a gate caught signature and type-contract drift | False confidence: tasks with real contract mismatches proceed as if clean |\n| The `implement-feature` orchestrator | Needs a real value to branch on at step 4a | Branches on a value that never arrives; must fall back to manual verification, defeating the delegation |\n| Operators/agents on the `work-backlog-item` grooming error path | Need failure diagnosis to drive escalation | The \"2nd failure \u2192 Escalation\" branch is unreachable; repeated failures never escalate |\n| Future `dh:*` agent authors | Need a truthful contract for where results go | `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` teaches the broken pattern, and `contract-verification.md` is its worked example |\n| Maintainers of non-Claude-Code harnesses (Codex, OpenCode, Crush, Kilo) | Shipped instructions must be executable on their runtime | Any SendMessage-based remedy is unexecutable for them \u2014 the constraint that motivated `11082f0eb` |\n| #3250's owner | A clean, non-overlapping scope | Collapsing the two items would make both unverifiable |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Risks\n\n**R1 \u2014 Silent regression after the fix (HIGH).**\nNo test file references `contract-verification`, its `<concerns>` output contract, or the\n`groom/error.md` diagnostic dispatch. There is no automated guard, so the fix must be verified by\nmanual end-to-end reproduction at both consumer sites, and a future refactor can silently\nre-break it exactly as `11082f0eb` did.\n\n**R2 \u2014 Fixing the agent but not the doctrine (HIGH).**\nIf `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` still describes \"finding is its response\" as a valid category, the\nnext agent author reproduces the defect. This is classified as a *recurring-pattern* item precisely\nbecause #3249 fixed one instance in isolation without closing the pattern.\n\n**R3 \u2014 Fixing one site and not the other (MEDIUM-HIGH).**\n`groom/error.md` is in a different skill tree from the item's title subject and is easy to miss.\nIt fails identically and silently.\n\n**R4 \u2014 Adopting #3249's fix verbatim (MEDIUM).**\n`SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\", \u2026)` is the most discoverable precedent and is wrong here for two\nindependent reasons: no team-lead exists in this dispatch context, and SendMessage is\nClaude-Code-only. The item, the fact-check, the alignment analysis and the user's own guidance all\nwarn against it \u2014 which is itself evidence of how attractive the wrong answer is.\n\n**R5 \u2014 Over-correcting into a new dispatch mechanism (MEDIUM).**\nIntroducing team infrastructure, a new transport, or a general framework to solve a two-site\nproblem contradicts the item's Small-to-Medium sizing and the existing in-repo precedent. The\nenumerated file set is small and known.\n\n**R6 \u2014 Absence still encoding two states (MEDIUM).**\nA fix that only records violations, leaving \"clean\" as silence, satisfies the symptom but not\nAcceptance Criterion 2, and leaves the gate indistinguishable from a broken one.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Open Questions\n\n**Q1.** Should the finding land in the backlog item's Concerns section (the destination step 4a\nalready names) or in a registered artifact (the `t0-baseline-capture` / `tn-verification-gate`\nprecedent, and the `subagent-contract` fallback)? Both are backend-routed and both are aligned;\nthe item does not decide between them. *(Planning decision \u2014 mechanism.)*\n\n**Q2.** Does the consuming step become a read-back (poll/read the destination after the agent\ncompletes) rather than a branch on a return value, and if so what tells the orchestrator the agent\nhas finished? Both consumer sites currently branch synchronously.\n\n**Q3.** For `groom/error.md`, is the correct resolution to route the diagnostic result through the\nbackend as well, or is the escalation logic better restructured to not require a dispatched\nagent's return value at all?\n\n**Q4.** How far does `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`'s doctrine change reach \u2014 is the \"finding is its\nresponse\" category qualified to the dispatch contexts where it genuinely holds (if any exist), or\nremoved outright?\n\n**Q5.** Is `contract-verification.md`'s line-151 override removed (falling back to\n`subagent-contract`'s artifact rule) or replaced with a dispatch-context-correct override? The\nImpact Radius frames these as the two candidate shapes.\n\n**Q6.** `contract-verification.md` still grants `SendMessage` in its `tools:` frontmatter although\nno instruction uses it. Should the grant be revoked as part of this change, to keep the tool\nsurface honest and remove the temptation to reach for the wrong precedent?\n\n**Q7.** Given R1, does this item carry an obligation to leave behind any executable guard against\nrecurrence, or is that deferred? The Root-Cause Analysis proposes a documentation-and-review gate\nplus a follow-up audit chore; neither is currently in this item's Acceptance Criteria.\n\n**Q8.** The item's Files list includes `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`, while the Effort section calls\nit \"likely a read/reference-only touch\". Is it in scope for modification or reference only?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Evidence Base\n\nAll claims in this document trace to the groomed content of #3276 (Description, Fact-Check, RT-ICA\nFinal, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Design Intent Alignment,\nReproducibility, Expected Behavior, Acceptance Criteria, Dependencies, Resources, Impact, Benefits,\nPriority, Effort). No research artifacts existed for this item (`artifact_list` count 0).\n\nIndependently re-verified against the working tree while writing this document:\n\n- `agents/contract-verification.md:151` \u2014 override text present as quoted.\n- `agents/contract-verification.md:5` \u2014 `SendMessage` still granted in `tools:`.\n- `skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a \u2014 bare `Agent(subagent_type=\"dh:contract-verification\", \u2026)`;\n  the synchronous \"If the contract-verification agent returns a `<concerns>` block\" branch; and the\n  `backlog_groom(section=\"Concerns\", append=True)` destination call.\n- `skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md:11` \u2014 doctrine sentence present as quoted; it is the only\n  occurrence of this phrasing anywhere in `skills/` or `agents/`.\n- `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \u2014 bare\n  `Agent(subagent_type=\"dh:task-worker\", \u2026)` requesting `last_success` / `first_failure` /\n  `failure_type`, consumed by a Mermaid decision tree branching on `failure_type`.\n- `agents/t0-baseline-capture.md` \u2014 `artifact_register` routing present, confirming the in-repo\n  precedent for this dispatch context.\n\nRT-ICA Final: all 7 conditions AVAILABLE, Decision APPROVED. No blocking information gaps.\n"}